WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.890
Back before X-Men, Spider-Man, and the Marvel
Cinematic Universe, the superhero genre was

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.280
a pretty tough nut to crack.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.320
Now Hollywood has mostly gotten it figured
out, but it didn't always used to be this

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:09.880
way.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.740
Here are some of the weirder early attempts
we could have gotten.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.240
Sam Raimi's Spider-Man series may look a little
quaint by today's standards, but when the

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.769
first film dropped in 2002, it hit like a
minor miracle.

00:00:20.769 --> 00:00:25.220
The flick enjoyed the biggest opening weekend
ever at the time, validating Raimi's lifelong

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.440
dream of bringing a comic book hero to life.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.169
His 1990 cult classic Darkman, with a superheroic
character of his own creation, was the result

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:36.230
of the director being rebuffed at every turn
in his bid to adapt an existing comic book

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.520
property such as the Shadow or Batman.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.740
After that film's relative success, Raimi
figured he had some creative capital to spend.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:47.700
When beloved Marvel mastermind Stan Lee died
in late 2018, Raimi shared a story about what

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.290
could have been with The Hollywood Reporter.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:53.190
"After I did Darkman, Stan Lee called me and
was like, 'Hey, kid, I liked your movie,'

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.340
He took me out to lunch and said we should
work together."

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.800
Raimi said he told Lee that he'd like to make
a movie about Thor, who had previously only

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.720
appeared in live-action form in the TV movie
The Incredible Hulk Returns.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.810
Turns out, the people who owned the film rights
to the character weren't ready for another

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:07.760
try just yet.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.180
"No!"

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.560
According to Raimi,

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.899
"We worked together writing treatments and
took it to Fox and pitched it.

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:23.820
And they said, 'Absolutely no.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.469
Comic books don't make good movies.'

00:01:25.469 --> 00:01:26.520
This was in 1991."

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:31.100
It would take Raimi another eight years to
land the gig of his dreams, and it's safe

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.400
to say that he acquitted himself pretty well.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:34.580
"'Nuff said."

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:40.860
As well-received as Sam Raimi's first two
Spider-Man films were, 2007's Spider-Man 3...

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:41.640
wasn't.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.920
Particularly problematic was the plotline
of Eddie Brock and the famous Venom symbiote,

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.450
which Raimi didn't even want to include in
the movie to begin with.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:53.420
But while the Venom storyline may have felt
pretty tacked-on, the movie as a whole does

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:54.479
have its moments.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.340
"How's the pie?"

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:57.980
"So good.”

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.660
Topher Grace's take on the Parker-hating Brock
was actually pretty good — the problem was

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.920
the character of the Venom symbiote itself.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.020
"I like being bad.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.060
It makes me happy."

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.680
Despite the negative reception to Venom's
live-action debut, Sony still had plans to

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.239
develop a spin-off starring Grace's version
of the character.

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:21.140
According to The Hollywood Reporter, the studio
was inspired to move forward with a more villainous

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.769
sort of anti-hero project after the rapturous
reception to Heath Ledger's Joker in 2008's

00:02:25.769 --> 00:02:27.060
The Dark Knight.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.420
Multiple script drafts were written for the
Venom spinoff project, but the whole plan

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.769
went out the window when Sony decided to reboot
the Spider-Man franchise and start over with

00:02:34.769 --> 00:02:36.220
the Amazing Spider-Man series.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.540
Who knows what Grace would have done with
a whole movie of his own to work with — especially

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.760
since he's since expressed confusion about
why he was ever cast for the role in the first

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:43.300
place.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.939
2008's Iron Man was the film that launched
the highest-grossing franchise in history,

00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:52.150
but Tony Stark's live-action debut could have
easily looked a lot different.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:57.220
By the time Marvel Studios was formed in 2005,
it had reacquired the rights to the character.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.090
But those rights had spent years being passed
around from Fox to Universal to New Line,

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:05.299
with names attached to the project including
Stuart Gordon, Quentin Tarantino, and even

00:03:05.299 --> 00:03:09.470
The Iron Giant screenwriter Tim McCanlies,
presumably just because of the word "iron".

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:13.959
In 2004, a script from the creators of the
Smallville TV series landed in the lap of

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:18.430
David Hayter, whose writing credits include
the screenplays of the first two X-Men movies.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:22.030
Hayter set about reworking the screenplay,
with contributions that included making a

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.370
villain out of Tony's father Howard Stark.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.780
Hayter's version went so far as to have the
character align himself with Justin Hammer

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.579
to become none other than...

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:30.829
War Machine.

00:03:30.829 --> 00:03:32.079
“Your dad!"

00:03:32.079 --> 00:03:36.530
Then-rights holder New Line loved Hayter's
version, and set the project up with an unconventional

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:40.959
choice of director: Nick Cassavetes, whose
biggest success to that point was the romantic

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:41.959
drama The Notebook.

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:46.349
Cassavetes was formally announced to be helming
the flick in 2004, with New Line aiming for

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:48.250
a summer 2006 release.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:52.480
The studio had reason to be optimistic; they
had spun box office gold from a Marvel property

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.810
with the Blade franchise, and thanks in part
to Hayter's X-Men flicks, superhero movies

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:58.720
had recently become surprisingly viable.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.290
But the project languished in development
long enough for Marvel to get Tony Stark's

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:05.499
rights back in 2005 — and the rest is history.

00:04:05.499 --> 00:04:10.909
Luke Cage, aka Harlem's own invincible Power
Man, wasn't the first black hero in comics,

00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:12.129
or even in Marvel lore.

00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:16.060
But he immediately earned a high profile by
virtue of a colorful costume, a formidable

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:18.750
power set, and an awesome catchphrase.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:22.060
"Sweet Christmas."

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:26.110
His inspired pairing with superpowered martial
artist Iron Fist sold a lot of books in the

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:30.229
'70s, but before Mike Colter put his live-action
stamp on the character with a self-titled

00:04:30.229 --> 00:04:33.800
Netflix series, only one honest attempt had
been made to bring the human wrecking ball

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:34.800
to the screen.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.070
In 2003, Columbia Pictures optioned the character
for a film adaptation, hiring scribe Ben Ramsey

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:41.410
to pen a screenplay.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:45.330
Attached to helm the project was John Singleton,
whose incendiary films Boyz n the Hood and

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:50.050
Higher Learning had established him as a talented
director capable of crafting masterful black-centric

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.610
stories that were accessible to a wide audience.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:57.050
His dream casting: Tyrese Gibson as Cage and
Terrence Howard as the villain Diamondback.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:01.840
The project eventually withered on the vine,
with the rights reverting to Marvel in 2013.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.610
But before they did, an even more interesting
name was floated for the title role: Idris

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.520
Elba, who told the Huffington Post in 2013
that he had briefly considered taking on the

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:10.040
part.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.320
The Flash is bound to get his big-screen due
with a solo movie any day now, but for the

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.540
moment, the Scarlet Speedster is mostly enjoying
a high profile thanks to his well-received

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.199
live-action series in the CW's Arrowverse.

00:05:22.199 --> 00:05:25.330
Warner Brothers has been attempting to get
some kind of version of a Flash movie off

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:26.930
the blocks since at least 2004.

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:31.449
It was around that time that audiences came
close to getting a version written, produced,

00:05:31.449 --> 00:05:34.419
and directed by Batman Begins screenwriter
David S. Goyer.

00:05:34.419 --> 00:05:38.430
At the time, Goyer called the Flash his favorite
of all of the DC properties, and shared his

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:42.020
plans to use the Wally West version of the
character, instead of Barry Allen.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:45.550
Goyer said his script was close in tone to
the Sam Raimi Spider-Man series, and that

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:47.880
he felt Ryan Reynolds would be perfect for
the role.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.240
But the project was cut short by creative
differences.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.320
By early 2007, Goyer had departed the project,
saying in one interview,

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.130
"The studio is heading off in a completely
different direction.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:59.660
I expect you'll hear of some new developments
on that front shortly."

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:02.120
The reality is: not so much.

00:06:02.129 --> 00:06:05.660
But considering how many writers and directors
the Flashpoint movie has gone through over

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:07.220
the years, it's not for lack of trying.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:12.660
Danny Rand, the immortal Iron Fist, has yet
to get his shot at a big screen adaptation

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.980
— and his ill-fated small screen outing,
which limped through two very poorly-received

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.180
seasons on Netflix, probably never should
have happened.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:19.700
"I'm the Immortal Iron Fist."

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:21.400
"Come again?"

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:23.100
"Sworn protector of K'un Lun?"

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.120
"What are you on, lithium?”

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:29.310
But in 2000, Artisan Entertainment, which
at the time held the rights to a number of

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:33.610
Marvel characters, was dead-set on producing
a feature vehicle for the relatively little-known

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:37.840
character, and the project made a surprising
amount of headway before biting the dust.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:42.270
Writer John Turman, who would go on to pen
Ang Lee's Hulk, as well as an unproduced screenplay

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:46.630
based on the Silver Surfer, was hired to write
the script, and Artisan even got far enough

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:48.550
into development to cast their lead.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:53.099
Scottish martial artist Ray Park officially
signed on to portray Rand in late 2000, and

00:06:53.099 --> 00:06:56.120
principal photography was slated to begin
early the following year.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.629
If you don't know him by name, you've still
definitely seen his face, at least underneath

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:00.919
a lot of scary makeup.

00:07:00.919 --> 00:07:05.099
The actor displayed a formidable screen presence
in the role of Darth Maul in Star Wars Episode

00:07:05.099 --> 00:07:09.190
I: The Phantom Menace, and also made waves
in X-Men as the flexible and grotesque Toad.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:13.849
But though Park was still talking about the
project as late as 2004, it simply failed

00:07:13.849 --> 00:07:14.849
to materialize.

00:07:14.849 --> 00:07:18.659
The rights eventually reverted back to Marvel
Studios, which briefly kicked around the idea

00:07:18.659 --> 00:07:22.870
of an Iron Fist feature in 2010 before the
fateful decision was made to go the serial

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:23.620
route instead.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:27.820
"Who is your master?"

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:29.560
"I serve only myself."

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:31.820
"Prepare to disappoint your master."

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.140
2018's Black Panther was the highest-grossing
film of that year and a bona fide cultural

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:37.860
phenomenon.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:41.780
Few people predicted the movie's incredible
success — except perhaps Wesley Snipes,

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:45.289
who had envisioned exactly that scenario over
25 years before.

00:07:45.289 --> 00:07:50.330
In 1992, the red-hot star was dead-set on
making Black Panther his next starring vehicle.

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:53.789
In a 2018 conversation with The Hollywood
Reporter, Snipes said,

00:07:53.789 --> 00:07:58.110
"Black Panther spoke to me because he was
noble, and he was the antithesis of the stereotypes

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:02.530
presented and portrayed about Africans, African
history and the great kingdoms of Africa.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:05.360
It had cultural significance, social significance.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:08.620
It was something that the black community
and the white community hadn't seen before."

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:13.220
Snipes tried in vain to find the right pairing
of writer and director for the project — a

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:17.370
task made even more difficult by the fact
that virtually nobody understood the character.

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:21.629
An ill-fated meeting with director John Singleton
illustrated that in hilarious fashion, as

00:08:21.629 --> 00:08:23.150
Snipes explained to THR.

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:23.900
"John was like, 'Nah!

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:24.420
Hah!

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:25.000
Hah!

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:28.580
See, he’s got the spirit of the Black Panther,
but he is trying to get his son to join the

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:29.840
[Black Panther] organization.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:33.219
And he and his son have a problem, and they
have some strife because he is trying to be

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:35.420
politically correct and his son wants to be
a knucklehead.'"

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.080
"He's not a Wakandan."

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:42.260
On top of people not understanding the then-obscure
superhero, CGI limitations of the time were

00:08:42.269 --> 00:08:47.180
also a factor, as the star was intent on rendering
Wakanda in much the same way as Ryan Coogler

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:48.180
eventually did.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.980
The project lost steam, but Snipes learned
from the failure.

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:54.050
Instead, he moved onto a different Marvel
Comics project: Blade.

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:55.050
As Snipes recalled,

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:59.310
"I thought, hey, we can't do the King of Wakanda
and the Vibranium and the hidden kingdom in

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.420
Africa, let's do a black vampire."

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:06.010
That groundbreaking project came together
wonderfully in 1998, and became one of Snipes'

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:07.010
signature roles.

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:10.500
"Some motherf---ers are always trying to ice
skate uphill."

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.900
Many attempts were made to bring the X-Men
to the big screen before the 2000 adaptation.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:20.200
Perhaps the closest anyone came to actually
getting it done was in 1990, when Stan Lee

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:24.140
and Marvel writer Chris Claremont pitched
an X-Men feature to James Cameron and his

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.140
then-wife, director Kathryn Bigelow.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:30.000
Cameron agreed to produce the flick, with
Bigelow set to direct, and a screenplay was

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.380
commissioned from Gary Goldman, the writer
behind Big Trouble in Little China and Total

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:35.380
Recall.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:38.850
That script, titled Wolverine and the X-Men,
would have seen Wolverine recruiting Kitty

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:40.860
Pryde for Charles Xavier's team.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:44.550
The story revolved around the group coming
into conflict with a presidential candidate

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:48.220
named Thomas Prince who, oddly enough, had
the powers of Magneto.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:49.600
Probably a coincidence.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:51.500
"Well, it's complicated."

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:55.500
Cameron's choice for the part of Wolverine
was actually pretty inspired: Bob Hoskins.

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.800
While Hoskins is far from the huge, jacked
man who eventually would come to define the

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.990
role on screen, it could easily be argued
that the stocky Who Framed Roger Rabbit star

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:04.839
had a more comics-accurate look.

00:10:04.839 --> 00:10:08.620
According to Claremont, the project went nowhere
largely because Cameron was more interested

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:13.680
in another Marvel property: Spider-Man, for
which he had a very detailed, and deeply strange

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:14.200
script.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:17.440
Of course, the director's Marvel dreams went
unfulfilled, but things still ended up working

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:18.880
out for him just fine.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:24.160
As hard as it is to believe, famous schlockmeisters
Cannon Films briefly acquired the rights to

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:28.570
Spider-Man in the '80s, when Don Kopaloff
— Marvel's film agent at the time — literally

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:31.230
couldn't get any other studios to bite.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:35.130
Cannon had big plans for their movie, eyeing
Tom Cruise for the title role, Bob Hoskins

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:39.529
for the part of Doctor Octopus, and Texas
Chain Saw Massacre filmmaker Tobe Hooper for

00:10:39.529 --> 00:10:40.620
the director's chair.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:44.579
When Hooper passed, they tapped Joseph Zito,
whose most successful previous credit had

00:10:44.579 --> 00:10:48.930
been Friday the 13th: The Final Chapter — which
is actually one of the better Friday the 13th

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:51.339
movies, though nowhere near the final one.

00:10:51.339 --> 00:10:55.300
Eventually, they cast stuntman Scott Leva
in the title role, and produced a teaser that

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.519
was little more than a proof-of-concept reel.

00:10:57.519 --> 00:11:01.230
Nowhere near able to actually make the movie
yet, the studio hoped to use the teaser as

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.180
an investor magnet to acquire funds to make
the actual film.

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:08.700
When investors failed to kick down the door,
producers slashed the film's budget in half,

00:11:08.709 --> 00:11:10.180
prompting Zito to depart.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:14.680
The subsequent failure of Cannon's notoriously
cheap-looking Superman IV: The Quest for Peace,

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:18.920
as well as the expense of producing the He-Man
movie Masters of the Universe, ended up sinking

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:19.620
the company.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:23.880
Cannon's Spider-Man slipped into limbo — perhaps
the biggest bullet the wall-crawler ever dodged.

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:27.060
